            <chunk name="Object" index="38">
              <items count="2">
                <item name="GUID" type_name="gh_guid" type_code="9">59e0b89a-e487-49f8-bab8-b5bab16be14c</item>
                <item name="Name" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Panel</item>
              </items>
              <chunks count="1">
                <chunk name="Container">
                  <items count="8">
                    <item name="Description" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">A panel for custom notes and text values</item>
                    <item name="InstanceGuid" type_name="gh_guid" type_code="9">24afd51e-9b73-54e3-b2e1-f12fa626d193</item>
                    <item name="Name" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Panel</item>
                    <item name="NickName" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">README</item>
                    <item name="Optional" type_name="gh_bool" type_code="1">false</item>
                    <item name="ScrollRatio" type_name="gh_double" type_code="6">0</item>
                    <item name="SourceCount" type_name="gh_int32" type_code="3">0</item>
                    <item name="UserText" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">RIBBED SHELL ROOF WITH DIAGONAL BRACING

Turns any freeform surface into a roof structure: a two-way grid of deep ribs follows the surface's U and V directions, and every bay between ribs gets a pair of crossing diagonal braces at the top of the ribs.

HOW TO USE
1. Right-click 'Base Srf' and choose Set one Surface to pick your own roof surface (a sample surface is already stored in the file).
2. Change 'Ribs U' and 'Ribs V' to set how many bays the roof is divided into in each direction.
3. 'Rib depth' sets how tall the ribs are, in model units; the braces sit on top of the ribs.
4. 'Brace radius' sets the thickness of the diagonal rods.
5. Right-click the Pipe and the two Custom Preview components, then Bake, to get the geometry into Rhino.

YOU WILL LEARN
- UV parameter space: Range + Construct Point make points in the surface's 0..1 domain, not in XYZ
- Iso Curve: a curve that follows the surface at a constant U or V
- Divide Domain2 + Isotrim: cutting a surface into a grid of sub-surfaces (bays)
- List Item with several outputs: picking the corners 0,1,2,3 of each bay to draw diagonals
- One vector (Unit Z x depth) reused for extrusion and for moving the braces up

READ THE CANVAS LEFT TO RIGHT
Green = inputs you change, coloured boxes = numbered steps, blue = results.

SOURCE
Lucas Becker (runxel) - runxel/grasshopper-examples
gh/shell_roof.ghx @ 0e57586bdc
Licence: BlueOak-1.0.0 (see LICENCE panel)
Modified for the Almond parametric library: regrouped, relabelled and annotated; the geometric logic is unchanged.</item>
